m2_alpha_llm/test: the scripted chat test

chat_tests.txt is the conversation to hold with the model to exercise
every tool, the plans, the squads and the contact set, with what to expect
of each line: chat_driver.py holds it for you against a live fleet and
grades it, and chat_test_md.py writes chat_test.md (one directory up), the
same script as tables for a person at the keyboard. Edit chat_tests.txt
only, then run chat_test_md.py (--check says whether the markdown is
current); the expect and issue lines are its prose columns.

  ./chat_driver.py                 headless, warp 10, every test
  ./chat_driver.py --gui           pMarineViewer up, warp 5, so you watch;
                                   after every test it asks you in the chat
                                   pane whether the boats did what you
                                   expected (--no-ask skips that)
  ./chat_driver.py --from 4.1      start there and run to the end; --to ID
                                   stops after that test
  ./chat_driver.py --phase 4,5     whole phases (the id's part before the
                                   dot); --only 1.1,3.1 names tests; --list
                                   prints the selection and launches nothing
  ./chat_driver.py --amt 2 --keep  two boats; leave the fleet up afterwards

The NEXT_TEST button (the viewer's fourth button in the run's copy, also
Action > next_test) always means "move on": pressed while a test waits
for the model's reply or for the boats, the test is cut short, graded on
what happened so far and marked "cut" in the results; pressed during the
settle it just ends the watch. The question after each test reads
"[test 4.3, auto PASS] Did the boats do what you expected?" where the
test moves boats, asks about the map where it draws, and "Did the agent
do what was intended?" otherwise (an `ask` line in the test file sets
its own): press NEXT_TEST for yes, or type a line starting with # and
what was off (a no, unless it starts with yes or ok). pLLMAgent logs such a line on
LLM_CHAT_NOTE and never sends it to the model, so you can annotate any
live run the same way. Your answer goes in the Operator column of
results.md, and every # line typed during the run, answer or not, is
listed in a Notes table under the results table by the test it landed
in; no answer within --ask-timeout (120 s) is recorded as such
and the run goes on. Alerts are off for the whole run except test 5.10,
which turns them on for one collision and off again.

ANTHROPIC_API_KEY must be exported: pLLMAgent reads it, the driver never
sees it. Each run copies the mission into test/runs/<stamp>/ (ignored by
git), launches abe, ben, cal on ports 9101.. with the shoreside on 9100
(so a mission of your own on 9000 can stay up), and writes results.md
and results.json there beside the run's LOG_ and XLOG_ directories.

fleet.sh here launches, inspects and kills a scratch fleet by hand (up,
ready, run, chat, plan, squad, dump, alog, down): plan copies a plan file
in, pokes it on a boat or a squad and waits for its end; squad creates a
squad and waits for its plan slot; dump prints review.py's tables on the
run so far. review.py turns a shoreside alog into the tables a review
reads (chat, requests and cost, plan states, Say lines, problems,
drawings with what is still on the map, first-step waits); views.py reads
the proposal drawings and previews; pairs.py prints every boat pair's
range over a window of an alog. These are what the headless-fleet and
review-run skills use.

How it works. The driver types each test's line into the chat by
posting LLM_CHAT_IN with uPokeDB; the chat pane shows such a line as
"uPokeDB> ...". It then reads the shoreside alog as it grows: a
proposal is an LLM_CHAT_OUT line from pLLMAgent:ask and is answered y
or n (the test's answer); a plan's Ask is a BT_CHAT line with the ask
mark and is answered with plan_answer; a plain reply ends the turn.
Then it waits for the test's wait lines (MISSION_ABE = goto_complete,
BT_STATE_RED = success ...) as NEW posts, grades the checks listed in
chat_tests.txt from the log lines since the test's mark (tool calls,
targets, the proposal's wording, reply length, collisions and near
misses, closest ranges, plan states, latency, truncation, the owners the
proposal's preview request named), and posts
the verdict as a text box at the top left of the map. Nothing older
than the test's start counts, so a value left by an earlier test never
satisfies a later wait.

Expectations must allow for the model's variety: "tool goto|route", a
sentence cap, a question mark, never an exact sentence. A failed test
names the check that failed and what was seen; the run's XLOG holds the
full story (LLM_TOOL_CALL, LLM_CHAT_OUT, LLM_USAGE, COLLISION ...).
